title: How We Use Terraform
page: terraform.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: Terraform makes the cloud match infrastructure described in text files, and the fastest way to get it is to trace a real, working configuration.

== The short version
layout: text
section: top
Terraform describes cloud infrastructure as text files instead of console clicks. You write what should exist, and Terraform works out the API calls to make reality match. Re-run it and it changes only what drifted.
notes:
Terraform is how you describe cloud infrastructure as text files instead of clicking around a console. You write what you want to exist, such as a bucket, a DNS record or an IAM role, and Terraform figures out the API calls to make reality match. Re-run it and it only changes what drifted.

The page puts it this way: the mental model is small, and the surface area is large. The fastest way to get it is to read a real, working configuration and trace what each block does. That is what this deck does, with CSOH's own Terraform, which lives in the repository under infra/terraform.

To follow along you need the Terraform CLI or OpenTofu, one cloud account, and comfort reading config files. No prior Terraform experience is required.

== Three verbs you will use constantly
layout: table
section: what-is-it
row: Command | What it does
row: terraform init | Downloads provider plugins and connects to where state is stored
row: terraform plan | Prints exactly what would change; nothing is applied
row: terraform apply | Makes the changes and records what it created in state
Read the plan every time: it is your safety net.
notes:
Terraform is a source-available infrastructure-as-code tool from HashiCorp. You declare the cloud resources you want in .tf files written in HCL, the HashiCorp Configuration Language, and Terraform talks to each cloud's API to create, update or delete resources until the real world matches your code.

init downloads the provider plugins your code needs and connects to where your state is stored. Run it once per stack, and again whenever you add a provider. plan compares your code to reality and prints exactly what would change. Nothing is applied, and the page calls it your safety net: read it every time. apply makes the changes from the plan, and Terraform records what it created in a state file so it knows what it owns next time.

== Declare what should exist
layout: code
section: what-is-it
You don't write "create a bucket." You write "a bucket named X should exist," and Terraform decides whether that means create it, change it or do nothing.
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "example" {
  bucket = "my-globally-unique-bucket-name"
}
```
notes:
This is what makes Terraform declarative. The page's absolute minimum is a single AWS S3 bucket: a terraform block that requires the AWS provider, then the provider block and the one resource shown here.

Run terraform init and terraform apply, and the bucket exists. Change the code and apply again, and only the difference is applied. Delete the block and apply, and Terraform deletes the bucket. That shape (declare, plan, apply) stays the same whether you are managing one bucket or an entire multi-cloud edge.

== Why it's great
layout: text
section: why-cool
- Reviewable: every change is a diff in a pull request
- Reproducible: the same code builds the same infra in a new account or region
- One language across 4,000+ providers
- The .tf files document what exists
- plan makes console drift visible
notes:
Your infrastructure is reviewable. Every change is a diff in a pull request, so you can see exactly what is about to change in production before it happens, and so can a reviewer.

It is reproducible. The same code builds the same infra in a new account or region, so disaster recovery becomes re-running apply instead of remembering what we clicked two years ago.

One language, many clouds. AWS, GCP, Azure, Cloudflare, GitHub and Datadog are among 4,000+ providers, all driven from the same HCL and the same plan and apply workflow. The CSOH site spans four providers in one repo.

It documents itself: the .tf files are the source of truth for what exists, with no stale wiki describing a console nobody remembers configuring. And drift is visible: terraform plan tells you if someone hand-edited a resource in the console. The page calls that a config-management feature and a security feature, because unexpected drift can be the first sign of a problem.

== Where it bites
layout: list
section: why-cool
Remote, locked, versioned state is not optional.
item: State is the whole ballgame
item: State can contain secrets in plaintext
item: The plan is a promise, not a guarantee
item: Provider upgrades can rename or deprecate arguments
item: Destroy deletes live infrastructure
notes:
Terraform tracks what it owns in a state file. Lose it, corrupt it, or let two people apply at once, and you get a bad day. The page says remote, locked, versioned state is not optional. State can also contain secrets: generated passwords, keys and connection strings land in state in plaintext, so it must be treated as sensitive and access-controlled.

Most of the time apply matches plan. Occasionally an API rejects a change mid-apply and you are left in a partial state, so small, frequent applies hurt less than big-bang ones. A major-version provider bump can rename or deprecate arguments: pin versions and read the changelog. CSOH uses ~> constraints and commits the lock file.

Destroy is real. terraform destroy, or removing a resource block and applying, deletes live infrastructure. Plan first, every time, and read the destroy lines carefully.

== Terraform or OpenTofu
layout: text
section: opentofu
In 2023 HashiCorp moved Terraform from the open-source MPL to the Business Source License. OpenTofu, a fork now stewarded by the Linux Foundation, stays open-source and is a near drop-in replacement. Everything here applies to both.
notes:
In 2023 HashiCorp moved Terraform from an open-source license, the MPL, to the Business Source License, the BSL. The community responded with OpenTofu, a fork now stewarded by the Linux Foundation that stays open-source and remains a near drop-in replacement: you type tofu in place of terraform.

For a project like CSOH's the two are interchangeable, and everything on the page applies to both. The page says Terraform generically throughout. If your organization cares about the license, the page calls OpenTofu the vendor-neutral choice, and switching is mostly a matter of swapping the binary.

== Code to cloud, tracked by state
layout: figure
section: anatomy
figure: tf-fig-title
Your HCL is read by init, compared to state and the cloud by plan, then reconciled by apply.
notes:
The figure shows the Terraform workflow from code to cloud. Your HCL is read by terraform init, compared to remote state and to the cloud by plan, then reconciled by apply, which updates both the cloud and the state file.

As the caption says, remote state is the memory in the middle. plan reads state to compute the diff, and apply writes state after changing the cloud. Lose or unshare that state and Terraform forgets what it owns.

== The terraform block configures the tool
layout: code
section: anatomy
It pins versions and says where state lives, so every laptop and every CI run behaves identically. The ~> constraint takes minor and bug-fix releases, never a breaking major bump.
```hcl
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"     # any 5.x, never 6.0
    }
  }

  backend "gcs" {            # where state is stored
    bucket = "csoh-org-495800-tfstate"
    prefix = "csoh/aws"
  }
```
notes:
Open infra/terraform/aws/versions.tf in the repo to follow along. It is short and shows the pieces that configure Terraform itself. The terraform block configures Terraform, not any cloud.

Three things to notice. required_version, just above this excerpt, refuses to run on an old CLI: this stack requires 1.6.0 or later. The pessimistic ~> constraint on the AWS provider takes any 5.x release, never 6.0. And the backend says state lives in a Google Cloud Storage bucket.

Yes, the AWS stack keeps its state in GCS. The page says that is deliberate: one state store to secure instead of one per cloud. The rationale is commented at the top of the file.

== Providers talk to each cloud
layout: code
section: anatomy
Notice what's missing: credentials. They come from the environment, a local AWS profile or a short-lived OIDC token in CI. allowed_account_ids aborts the apply if they point at the wrong account.
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region              = var.aws_region
  allowed_account_ids = [var.aws_account_id]   # safety tripwire
}
```
notes:
A provider is the plugin that translates HCL into a specific platform's API calls. You declare which ones you need in the terraform block and configure them in a provider block like this one.

There are no credentials here. They come from the environment: your local AWS profile, or in CI a short-lived OIDC token. That is why no secrets appear in the file.

allowed_account_ids is a guardrail the page says CSOH loves. If the credentials Terraform happens to be using point at the wrong account, the apply aborts before touching anything.

== Variables, resources and outputs
layout: table
section: anatomy
row: Block | Usual file | What it is
row: variable | variables.tf | A named input with a type and optional default, read as var.name
row: resource | s3.tf and others | One real cloud object, referred to as TYPE.NAME.attribute
row: output | outputs.tf | A value surfaced after apply, such as a role ARN
References between blocks pass values and set the dependency order. You never sequence things by hand.
notes:
These are the other three building blocks, each in its own file by convention. A variable is a named input with a type and an optional default, read elsewhere as var.name. CSOH's variables all have defaults, so the stack runs with zero manual input.

A resource is one real cloud object to create and manage, declared with its type and a name and referred to elsewhere as TYPE.NAME.attribute. An output is a value to surface after apply, such as a bucket name or a role ARN. CSOH feeds these into GitHub repo Variables that the deploy workflow reads.

References between blocks, such as var.bucket_name or aws_s3_bucket.site.arn, do double duty. They pass values around, and they tell Terraform the dependency order, so it builds the bucket before the role that points at it.

== Four stacks, one state bucket
layout: code
section: tour
Each directory is its own stack with its own state, under its own prefix in one GCS bucket. A bad Cloudflare apply can't delete the AWS bucket, because AWS isn't in that state file.
```text
infra/terraform/
  aws/          S3 (private) + CloudFront/OAC + GitHub OIDC role
  azure/        Storage account + $web static website + federated cred
  gcp/          Cloud Run + Artifact Registry + Workload Identity Federation
  cloudflare/   Load Balancer + pool/monitor + header/redirect/cache rules
```
notes:
The entire site is static, served active/active from three clouds behind Cloudflare. How We Deploy describes that shape; here we read the Terraform that builds it, in four independent stacks under infra/terraform.

Each directory is its own stack with its own state. You cd in, or use -chdir, and run init, plan and apply there. All four keep state in the same GCS bucket under separate prefixes, csoh/aws, csoh/azure, csoh/prod and csoh/cloudflare: one secured store, no per-cloud bootstrapping.

Why four stacks instead of one? A smaller blast radius. Each cloud also authenticates differently, plans faster on its own, and can be destroyed independently. The trade-off is coordinating outputs between stacks by hand, which the page calls a fine price for a four-stack, solo-maintained repo.

== Keyless deploys: the security centerpiece
layout: code
section: tour
GitHub Actions proves its identity with a short-lived, signed OIDC token, and AWS hands back temporary credentials. Only a workflow in this repo, running in the production environment, may assume the deploy role.
```hcl
condition {
  test     = "StringEquals"
  variable = "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub"
  values   = ["repo:${var.github_owner}/${var.github_repo}:environment:production"]
}
```
notes:
The page says the file worth reading twice is aws/oidc.tf. It sets up deploys with no long-lived cloud secret in the repo at all. GitHub Actions proves its identity to AWS with a short-lived, signed OIDC token, and AWS hands back temporary credentials.

Read the condition out loud: only a workflow in this repo, running in the production environment, may assume the deploy role. A token from any other repo, any fork or any other environment is rejected.

The role's permissions are equally tight: write to one bucket and invalidate one CloudFront distribution, nothing else. So even if a token leaked, it could touch exactly one site's files and nothing else in the account. GCP does the same with Workload Identity Federation in gcp/wif.tf, and Azure with a federated credential in azure/identity.tf.

== Pin the deploy job, not the whole repository
layout: code
section: tour
Trusting the repository alone would let any workflow in it mint deploy credentials, including scheduled jobs that read untrusted web pages. So GCP's attribute condition requires two claims.
```hcl
attribute_condition = "assertion.repository == '${var.github_owner}/${var.github_repo}' && assertion.sub == 'repo:${var.github_owner}/${var.github_repo}:environment:production'"
```
notes:
All three clouds pin the same subject string. Trusting the repository alone would let any workflow in the repo mint deploy credentials, including scheduled jobs that read untrusted web pages and never go near the production environment. "Only this repo" is not the same statement as "only this deploy job." So GCP's attribute condition requires two claims: the repository, and the full subject naming the production environment.

The IAM binding underneath names that one identity with a principal:// member instead of matching a group with principalSet://. principalSet:// matches every federated identity sharing a mapped attribute, and principal:// names exactly one subject.

The attribute condition is the hard gate that rejects a bad token outright, so the member is defense in depth. The page says having both say the same thing is what makes the file honest to read.

== Two lessons Terraform will not warn you about
layout: compare
section: tour
col: Mapping is not checking | attribute.ref is mapped from the token's ref claim | No condition mentions assertion.ref | So nothing restricts the branch
col: An unused variable reads like a control | var.github_branch is referenced nowhere in the trust | No plan or diff points at it | The config looks like branch enforcement exists
notes:
The page draws two lessons that generalize well past this one file, and Terraform will warn you about neither.

Mapping an attribute does nothing on its own. The attribute_mapping block maps attribute.ref from the token's ref claim, right next to repository and workflow. Mapping makes a claim available to match on later; it is not a check. A condition that never mentions assertion.ref restricts nothing about the branch, however prominently the mapping sits above it.

A declared-but-unused variable reads like a control. var.github_branch lives in gcp/variables.tf and is referenced nowhere in the trust. Terraform does not flag an unused variable, so no plan and no diff points at it, and this repo does not run TFLint, whose terraform_unused_declarations rule would flag it. It simply makes the config look like branch enforcement exists. The variable stays, because it does document the intended branch, but its comment now states plainly that the WIF trust does not read it.

== A resource, start to finish
layout: code
section: tour
AWS splits bucket settings across small resources that each point back at the one bucket. That reference both targets the bucket and orders the work.
```hcl
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "site" {
  bucket = var.bucket_name
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_versioning" "site" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.site.id      # depends on the bucket above
  versioning_configuration {
    status = "Suspended"              # S3's only "off" once enabled
  }
}
```
notes:
This is from aws/s3.tf: the private origin bucket and one of its settings.

AWS bucket settings are split across several small resources, such as versioning, encryption, the public-access block and the policy, that each point back at the one bucket via bucket = aws_s3_bucket.site.id. That reference both targets the bucket and orders the work, which is what the comment on that line says: it depends on the bucket above.

The value in the versioning block has a story of its own, which is the next slide.

== A sensible setting can be wrong for your bucket
layout: stats
section: tour
stat: 2.77M | about this many old versions | By September 2026, with nothing set to expire them
stat: 234 GB | in those old versions | Sat behind a 255 MB site
Versioning ran Enabled for months as a rollback trail, and every deploy re-uploads the whole site. None of the old versions were needed: git is the rollback.
notes:
A setting that is sensible for most buckets can be wrong for yours, and how the bucket gets written decides which. This bucket ran with versioning Enabled for months, as a rollback trail. But the deploy re-uploads the whole site on every run, so every deploy also kept one more full copy of it. By September 2026, about 2.77 million old versions, 234 GB, sat behind a 255 MB site, with nothing set to expire them. None were needed, because every deploy rebuilds the bucket from git, and git is the rollback.

The value is worth a second look too. Once a bucket has been versioned, S3 can never make it unversioned again, so Suspended is the off switch and Disabled is rejected. Suspending also leaves every stored copy in place, which is why the same file also declares a lifecycle rule that deletes them.

== Headers at the origin, not only at the edge
layout: table
section: tour
row: Layer | Where its security headers come from
row: Cloudflare edge | cloudflare/rules.tf, added to every response
row: AWS origin | A response headers policy in aws/cloudfront.tf
row: GCP origin | Its nginx image, from nginx-security-headers.conf
row: Azure origin | Only the edge: Blob static websites cannot emit them
A CloudFront distribution has its own public hostname, so an origin can be reached directly. The cost is the same values in three files that have to move together.
notes:
The site's security headers, such as CSP, HSTS and X-Frame-Options, are declared once at Cloudflare in cloudflare/rules.tf and added to every response on the way out. That covers normal traffic. It leaves open what an origin serves when it is reached directly, and the page says that is a real path, because a CloudFront distribution has its own public cloudfront.net hostname.

aws/cloudfront.tf answers it with an aws_cloudfront_response_headers_policy, referenced from the distribution's default_cache_behavior, so the origin sets its own headers. The GCP origin never needed this, because its nginx image already sets the same headers itself. Azure is the honest gap: Blob static websites cannot emit custom response headers at all, so that origin depends on the edge and no amount of Terraform changes it.

The cost of the pattern is duplication. The same values live in three files, cloudflare/rules.tf, aws/cloudfront.tf and nginx-security-headers.conf, and have to move together. The page calls that the usual defense-in-depth trade: maintenance you take on in exchange for a control that survives any one layer being wrong. It is live on AWS once terraform -chdir=infra/terraform/aws apply runs.

== Build redirect targets, don't rewrite them
layout: code
section: tour
The www-to-apex redirect builds its target from constants plus the request path. Rewriting the inbound URL would inherit whatever the client supplied, including the scheme.
```hcl
target_url {
  expression = "concat(\"https://csoh.org\", http.request.uri.path)"
}
```
notes:
The Cloudflare stack is the odd one out: no servers, just rules. cloudflare/rules.tf holds the rule that sends the www host to the apex domain. Scheme and host are constants. Only the path comes from the request, and preserve_query_string = true carries any query string across.

The tidier-looking alternative is to take the inbound URL and pattern-replace the www. out of it, and the page calls that a trap. Redirect rules run in the dynamic-redirect phase, before Always Use HTTPS gets a say, and the matching expression matches plaintext HTTP exactly as happily as HTTPS. A replacement pattern anchored on https would not match an http request at all, and a replace that matches nothing returns its input unchanged, which is a 301 pointing at the request's own URL.

Assembling the target from constants plus one specific field cannot produce that class of bug. This one lands at the edge after terraform -chdir=infra/terraform/cloudflare apply.

== Always plan before apply
layout: table
section: concepts
row: Plan symbol | What it means
row: + | Create
row: ~ | Change in place
row: -/+ | Destroy and recreate (downtime!)
row: - | Destroy
The recreate and destroy lines are where production incidents come from. In CI, save the plan and apply that exact plan.
notes:
Read the plan. A plus sign is create, a tilde is change in place, minus-slash-plus is destroy and recreate, which means downtime, and a minus sign is destroy. The page says the recreate and destroy lines are where production incidents come from.

In CI, save the plan and apply that exact plan, so what you reviewed is what runs.

== Habits that keep newcomers out of trouble
layout: checklist
section: concepts
check: Keep state remote and locked; never commit it to git
check: Commit .terraform.lock.hcl
check: Change infrastructure in code, not the console
check: Prefer for_each with stable keys over count
check: Know which stack directory you are planning
check: Import existing resources instead of recreating them
notes:
These come from the page's list of concepts that bite newcomers.

By default Terraform writes terraform.tfstate to your local disk. That is fine for a tutorial and a disaster for a team or CI, because two applies at once race and corrupt it. Use a remote backend, such as CSOH's gcs backend, and state locking, so a second apply waits for the first to finish.

terraform init writes .terraform.lock.hcl, which records the exact provider versions and their checksums. Commit it: it guarantees byte-identical provider binaries on every machine and catches a tampered provider via checksum mismatch.

If someone changes a resource by hand in the console, the next plan wants to put it back the way your code says. CSOH even tags every AWS resource managedBy = "terraform" as a visible reminder. count indexes by position, so removing the middle item renumbers everything after it and Terraform plans destroy and recreate. for_each with stable keys avoids that when items can be added or removed.

CSOH drives each stack with terraform -chdir=infra/terraform/aws plan and its siblings. Each directory is an isolated root module with its own state and its own init, and forgetting which directory you are in is the most common "why is the plan empty?" moment. Finally, terraform import, or an import block, brings an existing cloud object under management without destroying it. Letting Terraform create a duplicate is how people accidentally end up with two load balancers.

== ignore_changes can quietly unmanage a resource
layout: text
section: concepts
CSOH's Cloudflare header ruleset carries ignore_changes = [rules] to work around a provider bug. Tighten the CSP in Git, run apply, get a clean plan, and ship nothing. So a check outside Terraform verifies the live headers.
notes:
ignore_changes tells Terraform to stop diffing specific attributes. It is the standard escape hatch for a value something else legitimately owns, such as an autoscaler's instance count, and used narrowly it is fine. The sharp edge shows up when the attribute you ignore is the only one that matters.

The Cloudflare security-header ruleset carries ignore_changes = [rules], a deliberate workaround for a provider bug that returns the multi-header block in non-deterministic order and so produces a permanent phantom diff. But rules is the only meaningful attribute of a cloudflare_ruleset, so ignoring it makes the resource inert after creation. Terraform reports success, the reviewer has no signal, and the edge keeps serving the old policy.

The page generalizes it: ignore_changes on a resource's only meaningful attribute is equivalent to deleting the resource from your config, except the config still describes it convincingly. So pair it with a check that asserts reality from outside Terraform. CSOH's is tools/check_edge_headers.py: it parses the header pairs back out of rules.tf, requests the live site, and exits non-zero if any header is missing or has drifted. The deploy workflow runs it and fails the build on a mismatch. The check gets deleted when the provider upgrade lets the ignore_changes go.

== A security baseline from day one
layout: checklist
section: security
Terraform is a security tool when you use it well and a footgun when you don't.
check: Remote, encrypted, versioned, access-controlled, locked state
check: No secrets in .tf or .tfvars files
check: Keyless OIDC for CI, pinned to the full subject
check: Verify from outside Terraform what Terraform can't see
check: Least privilege for roles and provider credentials
check: Scan IaC with Trivy or Checkov before apply
check: Review the plan like a code review
notes:
This is the page's baseline, from day one. No local state for anything real: lock it, and assume it contains secrets, because it can. Pull credentials from the environment or a secrets manager, or, best of all, do not use long-lived credentials at all, and add *.tfvars and .terraform/ to .gitignore.

Prefer keyless OIDC for CI. CSOH's deploys carry zero static cloud keys, and the page says that if you take one idea from it, take this one. Pin the full subject, not just the repository, and remember that only the condition you write is enforced.

Anything you exclude from management needs a check that reads the live system and fails a build on drift. The page says a clean plan is evidence about your config, not about production. The deploy role can write one bucket and invalidate one CDN, not PowerUserAccess.

Static analyzers such as Trivy, which now includes tfsec, and Checkov catch a public bucket or a 0.0.0.0/0 security group before apply. The baseline also repeats two earlier points: pin provider versions and commit the lock file, and use allowed_account_ids or equivalent checks against the wrong account. Treat a surprising destroy-and-recreate or destroy line in a plan the way you would treat a surprising rm -rf in a pull request.

== Read the repo in this order
layout: timeline
section: using-our-repo
event: Tool config | aws/versions.tf
event: Inputs | aws/variables.tf
event: Resources | aws/s3.tf and cloudfront.tf
event: Security | aws/oidc.tf: trust policy vs permissions policy
event: Edge | The cloudflare/ stack: a different provider, same workflow
The Terraform is real, in production, and heavily commented for beginners.
notes:
The page's point is that CSOH's Terraform is real, in production, and heavily commented for beginners: every non-obvious line has a one-to-three-line explanation. This is its suggested reading order.

Start with aws/versions.tf: the terraform block, provider pinning and the remote backend in one short file. Then aws/variables.tf, for how values are declared once and reused as var.x. Then a real resource in aws/s3.tf and cloudfront.tf: resources, references, and how settings split across small blocks. Then the security set-piece, aws/oidc.tf: trust policy versus permissions policy, scoped to one repo and environment. Finally the cloudflare stack, a different provider entirely, driven by the same workflow, which the page offers as proof that the shape transfers across clouds.

infra/README.md ties it together with the architecture diagram, the bootstrap commands, the cutover and rollback plan, and the cost table (~$8-12/mo across three clouds). Fork the repo, swap in your own account IDs and bucket names, and you have a working multi-cloud static-hosting setup to learn from.

== Why pin the environment rather than the branch?
layout: question
section: tour
The trust names environment:production and never reads the branch. Consider what guards that environment, and what a branch check alone would let through.
notes:
This question comes from the page's tour of the OIDC trust. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== One string, two controls
layout: answer
section: tour
The production environment is restricted to main by a deployment branch policy, so pinning it enforces the branch transitively. It also adds a gate a ref check alone would miss: a workflow can run on main without entering the environment.
notes:
The GitHub production environment is itself restricted to main by a deployment branch policy. Pinning the environment therefore enforces the branch transitively, and adds a gate a ref check alone would miss, because a workflow can run on main without ever entering the environment. The page sums it up as one string, two controls.

As with any change to a stack, this reaches the cloud when someone runs terraform -chdir=infra/terraform/gcp apply, not when the file is merged.

== Does marking an output sensitive protect the secret?
layout: question
section: concepts
A resource generates a database password, and you mark the output that carries it sensitive. Consider where Terraform keeps the password, and who can read it there.
notes:
This one comes from the concepts that bite newcomers. Ask it before showing the answer.

== No: state holds it in plaintext
layout: answer
section: concepts
Any secret a resource generates is stored in state in plaintext, even if you marked the output sensitive. That only hides it from the console. So the bucket holding state must be encrypted, access-controlled and versioned.
notes:
Any secret a resource generates, such as a database password, an access key or a TLS private key, is stored in state in plaintext, even if you marked the output sensitive. Marking it sensitive only hides it from the console.

So the bucket holding state must be encrypted, access-controlled and versioned. The page says those are exactly the properties CSOH's GCS state bucket is configured with at bootstrap. The rule CSOH applies to all four of its stacks is to secure the state file like production, because it can contain secrets and it is the keys to your infrastructure.
